{
  "_": "SolariNet status panel — canonical FRAMEBUFFER-PARITY fixture (task PF1).",
  "_usage": "One dataset, read by BOTH renderers: status-panel/firmware/test/panelParityTest.c (C host harness, real panelScreens*.c) and tests/dashboard/test_panel_parity.js (real dashboard/public/screens-panel.jsx). `history` is chronological, oldest first, `cadenceSec` apart, in the GET /api/panel `data` payload shape that buildEnv() already consumes — so the JS side needs no translation layer. The C side decodes the same keys into a PanelSnapshot and replays them through panelEnvApply().",
  "_states": "systems[].state is the API's lowercase word (up/degraded/down/unknown/maint); pools[] and stateRoll use `up` for the OK count, matching panel-snapshot.json. gear[] follows CONTRACT-AW §3.1: role 0=router 1=switch 2=hub 3=ap 4=wanBackup; state 0=down 1=up 2=degraded (NOT the PanelState enum); rx/txLevel 0..7.",
  "_gear": "Every gear row carries txLevel 0 DELIBERATELY. §3.1 makes level 0 idle and BOTH renderers draw no particle at all for it, which removes the one part of A1 the two implementations do not share (see _particles). Everything left on the screen — gates, bands, legs, the internet and wanBackup columns — is a pure function of the fixture, so it can be diffed pixel for pixel. rxLevel is varied so the leg brightnesses are not all equal.",
  "_particles": "The two A1 particle integrators differ BY DESIGN and are out of scope to reconcile: the firmware carries 90 particles keyed to a source gate (panelScreensA.c A1_PARTICLES), the page carries 72 keyed to a leg (screens-panel.jsx A1_PARTICLES), with different speed and brightness curves. With txLevel 0 neither draws any, so the parity diff is not affected.",
  "_gearVariants": "gearRouterDown is the same inventory with the router DOWN, which exercises CONTRACT-AW §10 A-3: the internet column inherits the router's state treatment while wanBackup keeps its own and reads as the surviving path.",
